  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhanced partnership and improved multilateral cooperation to promote integrated policy responses for social justice through decent work in Brazil by the end of 2025</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ILO Office in Brazil is working to strengthen partnerships and multilateral cooperation to promote social justice through decent work during the 2024&#x2013;2025 biennium.

Efforts focus on advancing labour rights, reinforcing fundamental principles and rights at work, and expanding inclusive learning programmes to address the challenges of the future of work. In 2025, Brazil will hold state and regional conferences in preparation for the II National Conference on Employment and Decent Work, to be held in 2026. This process will foster social dialogue and help shape integrated labour policies aligned with international standards.

By the end of 2025, Brazil aims to advance the ratification of key ILO instruments, including Conventions C190 and C156 and Protocol 29. The Ministry of Labour and Employment (MTE) will also submit reports on the application of Conventions C100, C111, and C169, supporting the improvement of national labour practices.

The development of a new Decent Work Country Programme (DWCP) is underway, aligning with Brazil&#x2019;s priorities and the United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation Framework (UNSDCF). Technical cooperation will also focus on strengthening transparency and voluntary conciliation mechanisms.

In parallel, the Brazil&#x2013;ILO South-South Cooperation Programme, Social Justice for the Global South, continues to support developing countries in Latin America, Africa, and Asia-Pacific, focusing on eradicating child and forced labour, promoting equality, strengthening occupational safety and health, and expanding social protection&#x2014;contributing directly to SDGs 8 and 17.</narrative>
  </description>
